{
  "$comment": "The CoffeeJSON gear registry — the canonical slugs for the Gear `id` field (docs/spec/06-vocabularies.md § Gear registry). Curated DATA, not spec: adding an entry bumps nothing (docs/spec/07-versioning.md § Registry governance), ids are stable once published (correct a mistake by adding a new slug and aliasing the old, never repurposing), and the set is intentionally non-exhaustive — `id: \"custom\"` plus a label always works and is deliberately NOT an entry here. `roles` says where an entry attaches in a document — `brewer` for `recipe.brewer`, `grinder` for `grind.grinder`, `basket` for `recipe.basket` — and an all-in-one carries more than one. `category` says how it brews, so only a `brewer` has one. An entry names a product at the granularity a source names it: the family (`hario-v60`, `kalita-wave`, `vst-precision`), never its sizes, materials or generations, which a document carries in the Gear object's own `variant`. `aliases` are true synonyms of the same product — a vendor's own brand name for an OEM design (`turin-df64`), a shorter spelling (`vst-18g`) — never a family name pointed at one variant, and never another maker's product name. Product-specific entries are listed first; generic equipment classes are grouped at the end. `label` is the neutral display fallback; `brand`/`model` appear only where they are unambiguous, which is why the OEM DF grinders carry neither. Served at https://coffeejson.org/registries/gear.json. Brand and model names appear nominatively, to identify the products they name; their inclusion claims and waives no trademark rights — CC0 covers this file's data, not those marks.",
